"executive_summary": "This paper investigates the foundational interpretation of gravitation, contrasting Einstein's General Relativity (GTR) as a curvature of space with a Maxwell-like field theory in flat Minkowski space. The author derives the gravitational energy-momentum tensor from the Einstein equations using a field-theoretic approach in Minkowski space, demonstrating that for flat space cosmology, the negative gravitational energy exactly cancels the positive material energy, resulting in a total energy of zero. The study extends this framework to the Relativistic Theory of Gravitation (RTG), a bimetric theory that breaks general coordinate invariance by coupling the Riemann and Minkowski metrics. A key contribution is the analytical solution of a singularity-free version of the Schwarzschild black hole. In this RTG model, the metric components inside the horizon decay rapidly but remain positive, preserving the standard role of time and allowing for the possibility of escape, unlike the standard GTR black hole. The paper further applies this framework to cosmology, showing that the model naturally reproduces the ΛCDM model at late times while predicting a form of inflation at early times. The bimetric terms, associated with a positive cosmological constant, act to cancel the vacuum energy, ensuring the zero-point energy vanishes. The author concludes that while the field-theoretic and curved-space views are equivalent in standard GTR, the bimetric extension makes the 'field in flat space' interpretation more natural, as it explicitly separates the background Minkowski metric from the gravitational field. This approach offers a resolution to the black hole singularity problem and provides a consistent cosmological model where the total energy of the universe is zero.",
